-- ⚠️ THIS SCRIPT WAS WRONG. Kept as the record, not as a fix. DO NOT RUN.
--
-- It proposed:
--     alter table api_keys alter column id add generated by default as identity;
-- Postgres answered:
--     ERROR 55000: column "id" of relation "api_keys" is already an identity column
--
-- ── WHY THE DIAGNOSIS WAS WRONG ───────────────────────────────────────────────
-- I read `column_default: null` on api_keys.id and concluded there was no
-- sequence. For an IDENTITY column, information_schema.columns reports
-- column_default = NULL by design — identity is recorded in `is_identity` and
-- `identity_generation`, which I did not select. So the field I looked at is
-- exactly as null for a correctly-configured identity column as for a broken one.
--
-- That is the S-122 pattern applied to myself: A VALUE THAT LOOKS LIKE AN
-- OBSERVATION BUT CANNOT DISCRIMINATE. I had written the guard for this class and
-- then read a non-discriminating field and treated it as evidence.
--
-- The live column is in fact `GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY` — which is stricter
-- than BY DEFAULT: any INSERT supplying `id` explicitly would be REJECTED. Worth
-- knowing, and not the bug either.
--
-- ── THE REAL CAUSE (S-138) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- /api/v1/keys POSTed a column named `intended_use`. The live table has `notes`
-- and has never had `intended_use`. PostgREST answers an unknown column with 400,
-- `_sb_post` collapsed every failure into the string "Key storage failed", and so
-- the endpoint returned the same opaque 500 from the day it shipped. api_keys has
-- 0 rows: not one key has ever been issued.
--
-- scripts/supabase_all_tables.sql ALSO declared `intended_use`, so the file and
-- the table agreed with each other about being wrong, and nothing compared them.
--
-- Fixed in code (keys.py writes `notes`; analytics.py selects `notes`), in the
-- schema file, and guarded by tests/test_table_columns_match_the_code.py.
--
-- ── THE EXPENSIVE PART WAS NOT THE TYPO ───────────────────────────────────────
-- One word survived THREE confident diagnoses:
--   1. "anon lacks INSERT on api_keys"    — it does not
--   2. "SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY is missing"  — it was not the cause
--   3. "id has no sequence"               — this file
-- Each was plausible, each was invented to fill the space a generic error message
-- left empty, and each cost a round trip through the one person with console
-- access. A failure message that names no cause does not merely fail to help — it
-- FUNDS wrong answers. `_sb_post` now passes PostgREST's own message through; it
-- describes our schema, not the caller's data, so there is nothing to protect.
--
-- Second lesson, cheaper and just as reusable: the live catalog was reachable the
-- whole time through the Supabase MCP. Three rounds of guessing were spent
-- because I reasoned about the schema instead of asking it. When an authority is
-- one query away, inference is not the cheap option — it is the expensive one.

-- ── The query that should have been run first ─────────────────────────────────
-- select column_name, data_type, is_nullable, column_default,
--        is_identity, identity_generation          -- ← the two columns I omitted
--   from information_schema.columns
--  where table_name = 'api_keys'
--  order by ordinal_position;
--
-- Actual answer: id | bigint | NO | null | YES | ALWAYS
--                                          ^^^   ^^^^^^  the discriminating fields
--
-- And the check that would have found the real bug in one step:
-- select 'intended_use' as col,
--        exists(select 1 from information_schema.columns
--                where table_name='api_keys' and column_name='intended_use') as present
-- union all select 'notes',
--        exists(select 1 from information_schema.columns
--                where table_name='api_keys' and column_name='notes');
-- → intended_use false · notes true
